e a  quick rundown of my experience with the  series paper mario 64 which i played  first was  amazing the thousand year door was  amazing er  super paper mario i greatly enjoyed  however due to the game being so  radically different gameplay wise i  always treated it more as  sort of a spin-off game a mix between  super mario  and paper mario sticker star can go  for itself and color splash i never  played  this was a conscious decision by me at  the time because frankly the game just  gave me too much sticker star ptsd  you ain't fooling me nintendo calling  them cards is not gonna magically make  me forget that the cards are the same  thing  as stickers simply put everything this  game presented to me at the time just  didn't spark my interest  so i can totally sympathize with those  of you who are still on the fence about  giving origami king a try  60 bucks is a lot of money and if you're  a cheap bastard like me  you might want to save those benjamin  franklin's or in my case  roman architecture for something you  know you're going to enjoy and support  and to those people i say all the power  to you  however there is a special breed of  gamers out there  these rare specimens have made it their  mission not to just voice their distaste  of a game but to make sure that the  whole world knows it and seem to find  great enjoyment in making sure the game  fails on every conceivable level because  it isn't what they want it to be  even if you genuinely feel the game  isn't good the game is not worth a zero  guys  just the fact that the game boots up and  runs well is already worth a point  you know what i did when color splash  came out nothing  i just said nope not for me and i picked  up some games i  actually liked playing what a novel  concept right now i get that for some  people the new direction of the series  is disappointing  i do i too would have loved to see  another thousand year door type of game  but  continuously crying over spilled milk  and fighting tooth and nail to get your  points across  isn't doing anyone any favors there are  three  amazing games out there that are right  up your alley you can always go back to  and play again paper mario is just one  of many  many franchises out there and the world  is not going to end because  one series is no longer going in the  direction you want it to  in other news the world is going to end  throughout the course of my playthrough  of the game my reaction slowly evolved  from  all right to all right too  this game freaking rules you can have  rave parties involving trees  vandalize walls race around in a piece  of footwear  throw confetti around like a stage  magician play the wind waker  and of course take your aggression out  on helpless toads  while i didn't play color splash i do  think that the game looked amazing and i  don't think the graphics were ever the  issue for most people  sticker star started this whole paper  and cardboard aesthetic but whereas that  game looks bland and empty  origami king is just bursting with life  and color one single screen just  bombards you with all kinds of little  details  some might prefer the mix between the 2d  characters in the 3d environment seen in  the first two games but for me  this paper crafted world just works very  well and it's not like everything is too  abstract either  houses still look like houses furniture  like furniture and zonai dungeon statues  like zonai dungeon statues  and it's actually interesting to see how  these objects are constructed using  these materials  i don't think the environments in  thousand year door were bad by any means  but this world presented in origami king  just  makes sense to me and complements the  paper inhabitants  story-wise the game is definitely more  simplistic and this is one of the few  things i do think the older games just  flat out did better  pun intended thousand year door didn't  necessarily have the most complex story  but it's the amazing substories in each  chapter that made it so great from a  mystery on a train to a fighting ring in  which fighters have been disappearing  under suspicious circumstances to a town  of shadow people who are one by one  turning into pigs with each chime of a  mysterious bell  you name it what i do like about origami  king though is the fact that they did  away with the whole  collect the seven thingamajigs to win  game that stuff was getting old anyway  origami king is more of a point a to b  type of adventure you  are constantly following the various  streamers which have mummified peach's  castle  and while you could consider toad town  to be the central hub of the game  it doesn't necessarily feel like it  aside from buying stuff and collecting  extras you only really need to come back  here i think  two times if you're only sticking to the  main storyline whereas in thousand year  door you always went back to rogueport  in between chapters and figure out where  to go next from there which was fine but  origami king's approach isn't  necessarily worse just  different and it definitely beats the  hell out of the level select system from  color splash and sticker star  i will talk a little bit more about the  story in a later point of the video but  first i want to get the battle system  out of the way  this is by far the most controversial  part of the game and the only thing i'm  really conflicted about  on the one hand i kinda like it and i  commend nintendo for trying something  new  i'm not going to go in depth and explain  all the little intricacies about the  battle system there's plenty of videos  out there who will gladly lay it out for  you but the main gist of it is you spin  the arena to line up enemies in either  squares or lines  lines are for jumping squares for  hammering a good lineup results in your  attacks dealing 1.5 times the damage  there's weapons you can equip items you  can use and surprisingly enough  the game does have sort of a badge  system to increase your damage defense  or get some extra time to figure out the  proper lineup during each turn  it's not nearly as expensive as the  badges in a thousand year door but  hey it's there and then there's the boss  battles which last considerably longer  and all have their own way of  approaching the fight  some mess with the arena some have  devastating attacks which need to be  avoided or can only be defeated using  specific attacks or special moves  i never found the battles to be boring  or tedious to the point that it ruined  the game for me  and this is coming from someone who  absolutely sucks at this stuff  there have been more than enough moments  where i just couldn't figure out the  proper lineup and had to either spend a  lot of money to literally buy myself  more time or just settle with weaker  attacks than i care to admit  there is however one glaring issue and  one which i definitely side with the  critics on  the battles are not rewarding enough  there are for instance no experience  points  nintendo's stubbornness to not include  more incentive to willingly take part in  these fights  is one that will forever be one of  life's greatest mysteries  it really isn't rocket science here guys  if you're gonna have a turn-based battle  system you gotta reward  the player all you get is coins and  coins are not exactly hard to come by  even while avoiding most battles i still  managed to bill gates my way through  this game  i never even came close to running out  and i think nintendo was fully aware of  this problem  and it almost seems like they purposely  worked around it not by having a level  system or at the very least have enemies  drop good weapons or helpful items but  by making certain enemies  unavoidable this doesn't happen too  often so again it's not like you're  going to be forced into a battle every  few minutes  but it is kind of hilarious that this is  their solution to the problem  most of the regular overworld enemies  are painfully easy to avoid which  like i said seems like it was almost  done on purpose remember how in thousand  year door  you had enemies that just came flying at  you at mach 5 from off-screen and you're  like oh  [ __ ] are these piranha plants who spring  up all over the place  yeah compare that to origami king's  enemies  oh wait wait wait a second wait bro  go away although the game does lose a  few points on this i stand by the fact  that this is by no means game breaking  i never really played paper mario games  for the combat system  as someone who's played his fair share  of rpgs as far as rpgs go even thousand  year door had the most bare bones  stripped down iteration of a turn-based  battle system  and this was done on purpose the games  were always meant to be accessible for  everyone  as opposed to more complex rpgs with hit  points ranging into the thousands  party management jobs skill trees and  tons of gear with various status effects  paper mario has always kept it simple  and i don't remember  ever having to grind to be able to beat  it for me the biggest reason for picking  up a paper mario game has always been  the story the characters and the humor  which  origami king has in spades speaking of  story  uh-oh comedically large spoiler alert  from this point on i will be showing  quite some stuff about the mid to late  game so if you're still planning on  picking it up pause the video  play the game and i will see you again  in about 15 to 20 hours  first off the characters the characters  in this game  rock olivia alone is probably one of the  best companion characters in the series  including thousand year door  she's like that optimistic friend you  always want to bring with you on a night  out because you know you're gonna have a  good time  she's witty she's charming and managed  to get more than enough chuckles out of  me  but she's not there for just comedic  relief either as even she has her  moments in the story where she struggles  with something or is sad and depressed  and you are tasked with  cheering her up which i was more than  happy to do her dialogue is great and by  the end of the adventure you really feel  like you have bonded with her  aside from olivia there is a plethora of  characters which tag along with you for  a while  characters like bobby who has a great  character arc and kamek who is really  funny  especially when interacting with  characters like olivia and bowser jr  they aren't always the most helpful in  combat as you have no control over their  actions but they do  attack enemies when they feel like it  sure it might not be on the level of the  first two games where you can use their  special abilities and control them in  combat their biggest contribution is  complementing the narrative and just  like with olivia there's some hilarious  and even  touching moments to be had with them  seeing all these characters interact  with each other even in real time while  you explore the overworld  really adds to the charm of the game the  main antagonist king ali who is olivia's  brother  isn't necessarily the most memorable  mario villain ever  but it's still a big step up from color  splash which went back to bowser being  the main bad guy  and in origami king you actually have  that classic team up with bowser later  in the game and  he is funny as he's ever been some have  criticized ali's motivations for doing  what he does but  come on it's a mario villain not a metal  gear solid antagonist  it's not like there's a high standard  here he's just a very emo guy who wants  to take over the world  each chapter in the game sees you  visiting many different locations all  with their own look and feel and little  substory to go with it  not only that but origami king is a game  that just keeps on giving i was  pleasantly surprised just how many  different things this game makes you do  one moment you're racing around the  desert in a shoe next you're suddenly in  a quiz show  exploring an ocean and fishing for  treasures or you're in a first person  aerial battle shooting  bombs at incoming paper planes just look  at these little guys go  this is what they live for blowing  themselves up for the greater good you  gotta respect it  this game is like a party that just  keeps on going  speaking of parties confetti when i  first saw the trailers and gameplay  demonstrations i thought this confetti  mechanic was going to play a much bigger  role in the game but unlike color splash  and its paint mechanic the confetti  isn't used in combat  at all the only function it serves is to  cross specific gaps or get  coins and if you're ocd like me you're  gonna want to fill every hole in the  wood no  no no i shouldn't phrase it like that  repair every gap in the fabric of  reality just because it feels good to do  and you get coins for it in return which  is nice  other than that the confetti is just  there i think there's only  two instances where the game  artificially drains your confetti to a  point where you have to search around  for more in order to progress  aside from that it can literally be  found everywhere by bashing every tree  plant or flower which is funny because  at the end of each chapter the game  makes this whole big triumphant deal out  of the fact that your confetti bag has  now expanded  oh okay cool  thanks the same thing goes for the  thousand-fold arms which  outside of battles isn't even used that  much other than little interactions with  the world here and there  but in terms of battles i gotta say it  does feel good bashing away at an enemy  using the motion controls  yeah look at this this is brutal oh damn  the bosses as characters were definitely  not on par with the older games  but as i stated before the battles  themselves were entertaining for me  and somehow i find it kind of hilarious  that they are simply office supplies  each one does have its own personality  believe it or not colored pencils is a  snobby artist rubber band is a  flamboyant lover of theater and drama  and hole punch is a passionate disco  queen just to name a few  again they are no hooktail or grubba but  at the same time  they are nowhere near as bad and  uninspired as some people made them out  to be  and the way they all have their own  unique traits when it comes to boss  battles like taping the rings together  so you are limited in the way you spin  the arena or how hoe punch takes away  parts of mario's body and decreases his  total hp  makes each one a joy to fight in how  many games are you trying to get your  face back  and i don't mean metaphorically i mean  literally your face is gone  there it is now you have to go get it  back exactly  each battle feels like a puzzle you need  to solve and i  happen to like puzzles even though i'm  bad at them lastly and i think this goes  without saying the music is great as  always  there's so many different tracks in  different styles you can really tell  that the composers had a lot of fun with  this one  all in all i think the game is just a  great little adventure with a lot of  enjoyable moments  the series might not be what it used to  be but that doesn't take away from the  fact that it's obvious that a lot of  love was poured into this game  in fact i think the best way to approach  this game is to just forget that the  thousand year door exists  just take that game set it aside for a  while  and just try to enjoy what is here it's  not a bad game  on the contrary it's a not bad game aka  good  not every game has to be that big  blockbuster or lightning in a bottle  type of game  and just the sheer existence of the  thousand year door is not a valid  argument to hate on origami king it's  meant to be experienced as a standalone  fun little adventure  since this isn't a real review i'm not  gonna rate the game but i would put it  in the same category as luigi's mansion  three  just fun little experiences with some  clever puzzles  simple yet effective stories and some  genuinely funny moments  now if you'll excuse me i have some  whacking to do  [Music]  you

